  "id": "COMPOUND:980 - PROTEIN:6138",
  "verbalization": "Doxorubicin is implicated in the regulation of 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14 through a multi-step mechanism involving the compound [(1R)-1-[(5Z)-4-Bromo-5-(Bromomethylidene)-2-Oxofuran-3-Yl]Butyl] Acetate. This compound acts as a target of doxorubicin, which in turn targets GDM-1 and UACC-893, both of which are linked to the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14. The evidence suggests a potential pathway where doxorubicin, through its interaction with [(1R)-1-[(5Z)-4-Bromo-5-(Bromomethylidene)-2-Oxofuran-3-Yl]Butyl] Acetate, influences the expression or activity of the proteasome subunit. The shared target of this compound with the proteasome subunit implies a possible role in cellular processes such as protein degradation, which is a key function of the proteasome. The evidence is consistent with a mechanistic link between doxorubicin and the proteasome subunit, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated. The presence of multiple pathways converging on the same target supports the hypothesis that doxorubicin may modulate the proteasome through this compound, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the precise mechanism.",
